On April 5, 2026, at 03:00, he experienced a sudden onset of left-sided weakness and difficulty walking. He was brought to the ER, where a brain CT confirmed a right putaminal minimal ICH, along with old small infarcts in the right basal ganglia, left frontal lobe, and bilateral white matter. He was transferred to the general ward on April 8. An ENT consultation was performed for an incidental left sinus hyperdensity found on imaging.

Regarding his neurological status, as of April 15, his consciousness was clear (GCS E4V5M6), though some mental impairment was noted. Cranial nerve examination showed left facial palsy, a preserved gag reflex, and a midline tongue. Motor function in the left limbs was graded as "Fair" (approximately 3/5 to 4/5). Brunnstrom stages were Stage V for the LUE and Stage IV for the LLE. Regarding respiratory performance, he tolerated room air with an SpO2 &gt;92% and received Acetin for sputum management. Regarding swallowing performance, he exhibited a delayed reflex, decreased laryngeal elevation, and a wet voice. Oral trials with a semi-solid diet on April 9 showed no residue, but he was returned to NPO with liquid training due to a high aspiration risk associated with a severe productive cough.

During the hospitalization, he received physical, occupational, and speech therapy. PT focused on therapeutic mat exercises, bridging, standing balance facilitation, and chest PT (percussion and postural drainage) for sputum clearance. OT addressed sitting and standing balance, LUE strengthening (shoulder and elbow flexion/extension), and core/LLE active range of motion. ST provided Hawk exercises, the Masako maneuver, and training in the supraglottic swallow technique with IDDSI Level 0 (thickened water), utilizing a compensatory head tilt to the right. 

Regarding functional status, his bed mobility was independent, and he achieved independent sitting balance (dangling). Bridging required contact guard, and bed-to-wheelchair transfers required minimal assistance. He remained incontinent of bladder and bowel.

For these neurological deficits, he was admitted to our rehabilitation ward on March 10, 2026, for intensive rehabilitation focusing on swallowing, tracheostomy training, and bladder training.</span><font color="#000000"><span style="font-size: 17px; text-wrap-mode: wrap;"><br>
